.TH DASHBOARD 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.4.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dashboard \- Static single-page home PV monitoring dashboard with a
Prometheus proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dashboard
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Static single-page home PV monitoring dashboard (energy flow diagram,
energy summary and chart) fed by Prometheus through the bundled
stdlib-only proxy: the browser loads the static app from the server, the
app's queries go to \fB/prom\fR and are proxied to the upstream
Prometheus (\fB--upstream\fR). The front-end configuration is served as
/config.js (the SOLAR_CONFIG object; the battery panels are always on
\(em battery support is always enabled).
.PP
The HTTP surface is the tool's serving surface: the connection caps
(\fB--max-clients\fR total, \fB--max-conn-per-ip\fR per peer, extras
get an immediate 503), the idle keep-alive kick (\fB--client-timeout\fR),
the bearer token (\fB--auth-token\fR \(em mandatory beyond loopback:
a token-less non-loopback bind refuses to start, F21; the `?auth=` query
bootstraps the session cookie). The default bind is the loopback.
.PP
The JSON-lines control server (\fB--control-port\fR, talked to with
\fBdashboard-ctl\fR(1) and \fBdashboard-cli\fR(1)) inspects and steers
the running server: the live settings (the caps, the log surface), the
config reload, the graceful shutdown. With a control
token configured the wire is HMAC-signed: every command and reply carries
the full sha256 of its payload, keyed by the token (common/wiresec.py)
\(em a token-holding client verifies both directions, and over TCP the
signed commands are mandatory. The command string folds to the grammar's
printable 8-bit before the server decides on it (NFKD, then the
printable-ascii range only \(em \fBshöw\fR runs as \fBshow\fR); the mac
still certifies the client's exact bytes. A second, read-only token
(\fB--control-ops-token\fR) authenticates into the show/clear verbs only
\(em the ops shift against the admin key, both roles audited.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
listen port
.TP
.B \-\-bind
address to bind (the default 127.0.0.1 serves tokenless; beyond loopback
a token is mandatory — a token\-less non\-loopback bind refuses to
start, F21)
.TP
.B \-\-upstream
upstream Prometheus URL
.TP
.B \-\-prom\-prefix
path under which Prometheus is proxied; empty string proxies everything
.TP
.B \-\-static\-dir
directory to serve
.TP
.B \-\-config\-js
config.js to serve at /config.js
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients (pages + proxy); extras get an immediate
503
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle keep\-alive HTTP client is kept before being kicked
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP connections from one peer IP; extras get an
immediate 503
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the page, config.js and the
proxy — mandatory beyond loopback (a token\-less non\-loopback bind
refuses to start, F21); a request carrying `?auth=<token>` bootstraps an
HttpOnly session cookie, the raw Bearer header is accepted too; the
value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file
(a systemd LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-port
JSON\-lines control server target: unix socket path or TCP port number
on 127.0.0.1 (e.g. 15598); 0 = off. Talk to it with dashboard\-ctl.py
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token":...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer (root,
the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED) skips
the token; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run the show/clear verbs only (no set/unset, no request reload, no
shutdown) — the ops shift against the admin key, both audited; the value
may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a
systemd LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-trust\-uids
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-verbs
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the plain 0600) ($CONTROL_SOCKET_GROUP)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-server
serve the dashboard's own Prometheus /metrics endpoint (the uptime, the
request counters, the proxied query total, the client gate) — off unless
toggled (the live `set metrics enable` toggle), and a token is mandatory
beyond loopback (a token\-less non\-loopback bind refuses to start, F13)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-listen
the metrics bind address HOST:PORT
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the /metrics endpoint —
mandatory beyond loopback (a token\-less non\-loopback bind refuses to
start, F13); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-acl
clients allowed to connect to the /metrics endpoint: "any", "none"
(refuse everyone), comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like
10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16, or "file:<path>" — the standalone ip\-prefix acl
file (the gate object {"type": "ip\-prefix", "rules": [{"type":
"allow"|"deny", "prefix": ...}]}: the last matching rule decides, a
client no rule decides refuses; "any" = 0.0.0.0/0, the whole space);
checked at accept — a client off the ACL is closed without a reply
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent /metrics clients; extras get an immediate 503, 0 =
unlimited
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle /metrics client is kept before the socket times out, 0 =
the built\-in 5 s cap
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent /metrics connections from one peer IP, 0 = no per\-IP
limit
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-log
DSH audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald way);
the XDG state home (~/.local/state/dashboard/audit.log) when unset
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " N"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping 3 backups; 0 = no size
rotations)
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " N"
rotated audit backups to keeps)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the DSH audit trail
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level
log level; DEBUG logs every request
.TP
.B \-\-log\-queries
log proxied Prometheus queries at INFO level
.TP
.B \-\-log\-access
log every HTTP request at INFO
.TP
.B \-\-local\-time
log timestamps in the system time zone instead of UTC
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-path
the state dump: the live setting overrides (the toggles, the caps, the
log surface — the tokens apart) as one JSON, written 0600 with an
integrity hash (a restore refuses a tampered, unsigned or
group\-writable dump); dumped on the request dump command and the
graceful shutdown; loaded on start only with \-\-restore\-on\-start, on
demand with the request restore command
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-key
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
state rides encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode keystream,
authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and a
configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash; a dump path
without a key warns at start and on set; the value may be `file:<path>`
— the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential
rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the server starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — the
live overrides ride again instead of a cold start; without it the dump
loads on demand
.TP
.B \-\-config
config file — the set grammar: every line a `set <name> <value>`,
comments start with # ($CONFIG, then server.conf next to this script,
then the subproject root)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit
(nothing is bound)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
re-read the config file and apply what is live (the same path as the
control server's request reload); the listen address and port are bound
and stay.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown (the connected consoles get the daemon-exit frame).

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines ({"cmd": "..."} ->
{"status": "ok|fail|bye", "text": "..."}; TCP requires the token when
configured and refuses to start without one). Commands:
\fBset\fR/\fBunset\fR/\fBshow\fR/\fBhelp\fR (the settings: max-clients,
client-timeout, max-conn-per-ip, log-access, log-queries,
log-level, metrics acl \(em the /metrics IP gate at accept, the table
show \fBshow metrics acl table\fR \(em and the token lives: control token,
the ops token,
metrics token, each rotating live, the value never rendering \(em
\fBshow <secret>\fR refuses), \fBshow connections\fR
(the live HTTP clients), \fBshow version\fR, \fBshow knobs\fR
(the dictionary of the registry), \fBinspect\fR,
\fBrequest reload-config\fR (the SIGHUP path), \fBrequest reload\fR (the
whole-daemon restart that keeps the sets: the live overrides save to a
state file beside the config, the process re-execs, the fresh daemon
re-applies them once), with a dump path configured (\fB--dump-path\fR):
\fBrequest dump\fR / \fBrequest restore\fR (the overrides as one 0600
integrity-hashed JSON; \fB--dump-key\fR seals it \(em the state rides
encrypted, a restore refuses a wrong or missing key;
\fB--restore-on-start\fR loads it at start),
\fBshutdown\fR, \fBquit\fR. The control listener rides the family's
live rebind: \fBset control port\fR (a unix path, a TCP port on
127.0.0.1, or off) swaps the socket without dropping the connected
sessions \(em a tokenless TCP bind still refuses (F5) \(em and the
family's control knobs all set live: \fBcontrol ops verbs\fR (the
read-only role's policy), \fBcontrol trust-uids\fR (the peer-cred
list), \fBcontrol socket-group\fR (0660 instead of 0600, the live
socket re-modes). See
.BR dashboard-ctl (1)
and
.BR dashboard-cli (1).

.SH SECURITY
The page and the proxy speak the F13 shape (F21 closed): the bearer token
(\fB--auth-token\fR, constant-time) is mandatory beyond loopback \(em a
token-less non-loopback bind refuses to start \(em and the loopback bind
serves tokenless; the `?auth=<token>` query exchanges for an HttpOnly
session cookie, the raw Bearer header stays for scripts. The dashboard's
own /metrics endpoint (when \fB--metrics-server\fR serves it) speaks the
family's one stack: the F13 refusal, the \fB--metrics-acl\fR IP gate at
accept (a client off the ACL is closed without a reply), the caps.
Terminate TLS at
an authenticating reverse proxy for anything beyond localhost (plain HTTP
carries the token in the clear \(em the F14 answer). The
control surface is loopback-only (TCP) or a 0600 unix socket (0660 with
\fB--control-socket-group\fR); a TCP
control port without a token refuses to start (F5); with a token the wire
is signed and the commands/replies verify end to end; a second, read-only
ops token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) scopes its sessions to the
show/clear verbs. The audit trail (in the XDG state home when
\fB--audit-log\fR is unset, prefix DSH) records the control commands with
their roles, the auth failures, the reloads and the shutdowns.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/dashboard-<port>.socket
the control socket used when \fB--control-port\fR is unset.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/dashboard/audit.log
the audit trail when \fB--audit-log\fR is unset.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dashboard-ctl (1),
.BR dashboard-cli (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1),
.BR modbus-cache (1),
.BR modbus-fake-inverter (1)
.PP
dashboard/README.md in the suite checkout \(em the full documentation.
